  "answer_text": "It looks like you're referring to a **GMAT Sentence Correction question**! Here is a full breakdown:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udcdd GMAT Sentence Correction Question\n\n**Full sentence:**\n\"Unable to build nests or care for their young, a female cowbird lays up to 40 eggs a year in the nests of other birds, including warblers, vireos, flycatchers, and thrushes.\"\n\n---\n\n### Answer Choices:\n\n- **A.** a female cowbird lays up to 40 eggs a year in the nests of other birds, including\n- **B.** a female cowbird will use the nests of other birds to lay up to 40 eggs a year, including those of\n- **C.** female cowbirds use the nests of other birds to lay up to 40 eggs a year, including those of\n- **D.** female cowbirds lay up to 40 eggs a year in the nests of other birds, including\n- **E.** up to 40 eggs a year are laid by female cowbirds in the nests of other birds, including\n\n\n\u2705 **Correct Answer: D**\n\n---\n\n### Explanation:\n\n**Why A and B are wrong:**\nIn A and B, \"their\" (plural) does not agree with \"a female cowbird\" (singular).\n\n**Why C is wrong:**\nIn C, \"female cowbirds...lay up to 40 eggs a year, including **those** of warblers\" \u2014 here, \"those\" seems to refer to eggs, yielding the meaning that female cowbirds lay the eggs *of* warblers, which is incorrect.\n\n**Why E is wrong:**\nE is out due to incorrect modification.\n\n**Why D is correct:**\nThe first major problem is the modifier \"Unable to build nests or care for their young.\" This modifier must be followed by **\"female cowbirds\"**. Notice that the modifier says \"care for **their** young,\" so it needs the **plural** form of cowbird.\n\n**D** correctly uses the plural \"female cowbirds,\" agrees with \"their,\" and uses \"in the nests of other birds, including\" \u2014 which clearly and correctly modifies the birds whose nests the cowbirds use.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc26 Fun Fact:\nThis question is based on a real behavior! Female cowbirds are known as **brood parasites** \u2014 they lay their eggs in the nests of other bird species, leaving those host birds to raise their young for them.",
